{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And you shall return unto HaShem your Elohim, etc., with all your heart and with all your soul, and HaShem your Elohim will bring back your captivity.</b> You already know the saying of our Rabbis of blessed memory in the first chapter of Avodah Zarah: the son of David does not come until all the souls in the body are exhausted, as it says (Isaiah 57:16): ״כי רוח מלפני יעטוף ונשמות אני עשיתי״ — \"For the spirit would faint before Me, and the souls which I have made.\" And the explanation of the verse is that He does not forever contend with Israel, nor eternally rage so as not to redeem you: ״כי רוח מלפני יעטוף״ — for the spirit enwraps from before Him, that is to say, what redeems them is on account of the spirits that go forth from before Him, ״ונשמות אני עשיתי״ — and the souls which I have made, which delay entering the body. And the word יעטוף is from the root meaning 'behind,' from the language of (Genesis 30:42): ״העטופים ללבן״ — \"those enwrapped belonging to Lavan.\"" "<b>And the Ramban, of blessed memory, explained</b> that for this reason the two verses were joined one to the other — the word נפשך ('your soul') to the word ושב ('and you shall return') — to clarify that when you return with all your soul unto HaShem your Elohim, that is, that all the souls of Israel that are in the body shall return unto HaShem and the new ones will merit to go forth; ״ושב ה' אלהיך את שבותך״ — and HaShem your Elohim will bring back your captivity — ״ורחמך ושב וקבצך״ — and He will have compassion on you and return and gather you; and this is what it says: ״אתה ובניך״ — you and your children — which is an allusion to all of Israel. And so further in Yevamot, in the chapter הבא על יבמתו, it says: the son of David does not come until all the souls in the body are exhausted, as it says: ״כי רוח מלפני יעטוף ונשמות״ and so forth; and it is written immediately after it (Isaiah 57:14): ״ואמר סלו סלו פנו דרך״ — \"And one said: clear, clear, make way.\"" "<b>And He will return and gather you from all the peoples.</b> I have already mentioned that the preceding parashah alludes to the First Temple, which was destroyed on account of the sin of idolatry, and therefore it warns concerning idolatry and punishes it with exile to another land — and the banished ones of the ten tribes who were exiled by the king of Assyria to Halah and Habor, the river of Gozan, and the cities of Media. And this parashah adjacent to it proves the same, for it speaks of the exile of the ten tribes; and therefore it begins with the banished ones and says: ״אשר הדיחך ה' אלהיך שמה״ — whither HaShem your Elohim has banished you — for with them, who are the majority of Israel, it speaks. And after that gathering, it mentions yet another gathering of a different exile, and they are the scattered ones — we here of Judah and Benjamin — who are subjugated under the enemies and haters who pursued us, and we are dispersed and scattered in their lands; and of this it says: ״אשר הפיצך ה' אלהיך שמה״ — whither HaShem your Elohim has scattered you — for with us it speaks. And it mentioned ושב twice, to allude to the ingathering of two exiles, which are two gatherings: the exile of the ten tribes and the exile of Judah and Benjamin. And of this the prophet said (Isaiah 56:8): ״עוד אקבץ עליו לנקבציו״ — \"Yet will I gather others to him, besides those of his who are gathered\" — for besides the ingathering of the banished ones, there is yet another gathering upon those already gathered, and they are Judah and Benjamin. Therefore it appears from the language of the verse that the essential repentance is destined to be among them, and this is what it says: ״אשר הדיחך״ — whither He has banished you — ״ושבת״ — and you shall return — for with them, who are banished, He speaks in the promise of repentance; and after they are redeemed, He adds: ״ושב וקבצך״ — and He will return and gather you — a second gathering, to allude to our redemption, we who are Judah and Benjamin the scattered ones; and of this it says: ״אשר הפיצך״ — whither He has scattered you. And after that, He returns and speaks with the banished ones as at the beginning; this is what it says: ״אם יהיה נדחך בקצה השמים״ — if your banished one be at the farthest end of the heavens — He promised them that He would bring them into the Land and they would inherit it. And in this He alluded to three inheritances, and in the third their merit will grow greater than the merit of the generation of David and of Solomon, peace be upon him; and He further promised them the annulment of the evil inclination and the removal of jealousy and hatred, and this is what it says: ״והביאך ה' אלהיך אל הארץ אשר ירשו אבותיך וירשתה והיטבך והרבך מאבותיך״ — and HaShem your Elohim will bring you into the land which your fathers inherited and you shall inherit it, and He will do you good and multiply you above your fathers; and it says further: ״ומל ה' אלהיך את לבבך ואת לבב זרעך״ — and HaShem your Elohim will circumcise your heart and the heart of your seed. And the sense of ״ואתה תשוב״ — and you shall return — is a promise that they are destined to do thus, that they shall return in repentance."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:52:20.489541986Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}